      "content": "# MeshCoder: LLM-Powered Structured Mesh Code Generation from Point Clouds\n\n[![Project Website](https://img.shields.io/badge/MeshCoder-Project%20Page-4CAF50?logo=googlechrome&logoColor=white)](https://daibingquan.github.io/MeshCoder)\n[![Hugging Face Paper](https://img.shields.io/badge/%F0%9F%A4%97%20Hugging%20Face-Paper-red)](https://huggingface.co/papers/2508.14879)\n[![Hugging Face Model](https://img.shields.io/badge/%F0%9F%A4%97%20Hugging%20Face-Model-yellow)](https://huggingface.co/InternRobotics/MeshCoder)\n[![Hugging Face Dataset](https://img.shields.io/badge/%F0%9F%A4%97%20Hugging%20Face-Dataset-blue)](https://huggingface.co/datasets/InternRobotics/MeshCoderDataset)\n\n[Bingquan Dai*](https://openreview.net/profile?id=%7EBingQuan_Dai1), [Li Ray Luo*](https://github.com/CreativeLuo), [Qihong Tang](https://openreview.net/profile?id=%7EQihong_Tang1),\n[Jie Wang](https://roywangj.github.io/), [Xinyu Lian](https://openreview.net/profile?id=~Xinyu_Lian1), [Hao Xu](https://hoytxu.me/), [Minghan Qin](https://minghanqin.github.io/), [Xudong Xu](https://sheldontsui.github.io/), [Bo Dai](https://daibo.info/), [Haoqian Wang<sup>†</sup>](https://www.sigs.tsinghua.edu.cn/whq_en/main.htm), [Zhaoyang Lyu<sup>†</sup>](https://zhaoyanglyu.github.io/), [Jiangmiao Pang](https://oceanpang.github.io/) <br />\n\n\\* Equal contribution <br />\n<sup>†</sup> Corresponding author <br />\nProject lead: Zhaoyang Lyu\n\n---\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [What is MeshCoder?](#what-is-meshcoder)\n- [Key Features](#key-features)\n- [News](#news)\n- [Quick Start](#quick-start)\n- [Installation](#installation)\n- [Use Cases](#use-cases)\n  - [Point Cloud to Code Conversion](#1-point-cloud-to-code-conversion)\n  - [Shape Editing via Code](#2-shape-editing-via-code)\n  - [Batch Processing](#3-batch-processing)\n- [Inference Guide](#inference-guide)\n  - [Data Preparation](#data-preparation)\n  - [Single GPU Inference](#single-gpu-inference)\n  - [Multi-GPU Inference](#multi-gpu-inference)\n- [Training Guide](#training-guide)\n- [Advanced Topics](#advanced-topics)\n- [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)\n- [Join Us](#join-us)\n- [Citation](#citation)\n- [Acknowledgement](#acknowledgement)\n\n---\n\n## What is MeshCoder?\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n<img src=\"figures/teaser.jpg\" width=\"100%\" height=\"auto\" class=\"center\">\n</p>\n\nMeshCoder is an AI-powered framework that transforms **3D point clouds into editable Blender Python scripts**. Unlike traditional 3D reconstruction methods that output fixed meshes, MeshCoder generates **parametric code** that allows you to:\n\n- **Reconstruct** complex 3D objects from point cloud scans\n- **Edit** shapes programmatically by modifying the generated code\n- **Understand** the structure and composition of 3D objects through human-readable scripts\n\nThe system uses a multimodal Large Language Model (LLM) trained on 1 million object-code pairs across 41 categories, enabling high-quality reconstruction with semantic part segmentation.\n\n---\n\n## Key Features\n\n- **Point Cloud to Code**: Convert raw 3D point clouds into executable Blender Python scripts\n- **Editable Output**: Modify object dimensions, topology, and structure by editing generated code\n- **Part Segmentation**: Automatically identifies and labels object parts (e.g., table legs, chair back)\n- **Large-Scale Training**: Trained on 1M+ object-code pairs for robust performance\n- **Multi-GPU Support**: Efficient inference and training on single or multiple GPUs\n- **Generalization**: Works on both in-distribution (Infinigen) and out-of-distribution (TRELLIS) data\n\n---\n\n## News\n\n**[2025.11.11]** We release the training and inference code, the pretrained checkpoint of the object-to-code model, and 100K training data of paired object and code.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nGet started with MeshCoder in just a few steps:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Clone the repository\ngit clone https://github.com/InternRobotics/MeshCoder.git\ncd MeshCoder\n\n# 2. Install dependencies (see Installation section for details)\nconda install pytorch==2.4.1 torchvision==0.19.1 torchaudio==2.4.1 pytorch-cuda=11.8 -c pytorch -c nvidia -y\npip install -e .\n\n# 3. Download pretrained models from Hugging Face\n# Visit: https://huggingface.co/InternRobotics/MeshCoder\n\n# 4. Download sample data\n# Visit: https://huggingface.co/datasets/InternRobotics/MeshCoderDataset\n\n# 5. Run inference on a point cloud\ncd recipes/inference/local_inference/custom_inference\npython inference_shape2code_point_input.py \\\n    --model_name MeshCoder/llama-3-models/Llama3.2-1B \\\n    --peft_model MeshCoder/llama-3-models/object_to_code_model \\\n    --npz_data_file 'data/sample.npz' \\\n    --code_save_file 'res/code.py' \\\n    --batch_size 8 \\\n    --visualization\n```\n\n---\n\n## Installation\n\n### System Requirements\n\n- **Python**: 3.10\n- **PyTorch**: 2.4.1\n- **CUDA**: 11.8\n- **GPU**: NVIDIA GPU with CUDA support recommended\n- **OS**: Linux (tested on Ubuntu)\n\n### Step-by-Step Installation\n\n#### 1. Clone the Repository\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/InternRobotics/MeshCoder.git\ncd MeshCoder\n```\n\n#### 2. Create Conda Environment (Recommended)\n\n```bash\nconda create -n meshcoder python=3.10 -y\nconda activate meshcoder\n```\n\n#### 3. Install PyTorch and Dependencies\n\n```bash\n# Install PyTorch with CUDA 11.8\nconda install pytorch==2.4.1 torchvision==0.19.1 torchaudio==2.4.1 pytorch-cuda=11.8 -c pytorch -c nvidia -y\n\n# Install PyTorch3D dependencies\nconda install -c fvcore -c iopath -c conda-forge fvcore iopath -y\nconda install pytorch3d -c pytorch3d\nconda install typeguard\n```\n\n#### 4. Install Additional Python Packages\n\n```bash\n# Install torch-scatter\npip install torch-scatter -f https://data.pyg.org/whl/torch-2.4.1+cu118.html\n\n# Install Blender Python API\npip install bpy==4.0.0\n\n# Install other dependencies\npip install opencv-python trimesh jaxtyping einops omegaconf scikit-image wandb mathutils boto3\n\n# Install xformers\npip install -U xformers==0.0.28 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu118 --no-deps\n\n# Install MeshCoder package\npip install -e .\n```\n\n**Note:** If `pip install bpy==4.0.0` fails, manually download the wheel file:\n```bash\nwget https://download.blender.org/pypi/bpy/bpy-4.0.0-cp310-cp310-manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl\npip install bpy-4.0.0-cp310-cp310-manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl\n```\n\n#### 5. Download Pretrained Models\n\nVisit [MeshCoder on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/InternRobotics/MeshCoder) and download:\n\n- Shape tokenizer: `shape_tokenizer.pt`\n- LoRA weights: `adapter_model.safetensors`, `adapter_config.json`\n- Configuration: `config.yaml`\n\nPlace these files in: `MeshCoder/llama-3-models/object_to_code_model/`\n\n#### 6. Download Base LLM Model\n\nDownload **Llama-3.2-1B** from Hugging Face and place it in: `MeshCoder/llama-3-models/Llama3.2-1B/`\n\n---\n\n## Use Cases\n\n### 1. Point Cloud to Code Conversion\n\n**Scenario:** You have a 3D scan (point cloud) of a chair and want to convert it into editable Blender code.\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1. **Prepare your point cloud data** in NPZ format:\n   ```python\n   import numpy as np\n   \n   # Assume you have a point cloud with Nx3 points and Nx3 normals\n   # Normalize points to range [-1, 1]\n   np.savez('my_chair.npz', points=points, normals=normals)\n   ```\n\n2. **Run inference:**\n   ```bash\n   cd recipes/inference/local_inference/custom_inference\n   python inference_shape2code_point_input.py \\\n       --model_name MeshCoder/llama-3-models/Llama3.2-1B \\\n       --peft_model MeshCoder/llama-3-models/object_to_code_model \\\n       --npz_data_file 'my_chair.npz' \\\n       --code_save_file 'chair_code.py' \\\n       --batch_size 1 \\\n       --visualization\n   ```\n\n3. **Output:** You'll get `chair_code.py` containing Blender Python code that reconstructs the chair.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Shape Editing via Code\n\n**Scenario:** After generating code for a table, you want to make the legs thicker or change the tabletop dimensions.\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1. **Generate initial code** (as shown in Use Case 1)\n\n2. **Open the generated code** (`table_code.py`) and find parameters:\n   ```python\n   # Original generated code (example)\n   leg_radius = 0.05\n   tabletop_size = [1.2, 0.8, 0.05]\n   ```\n\n3. **Edit parameters** to your liking:\n   ```python\n   # Modified code\n   leg_radius = 0.08  # Thicker legs\n   tabletop_size = [1.5, 1.0, 0.05]  # Larger tabletop\n   ```\n\n4. **Execute the modified code** in Blender to see your changes:\n   ```bash\n   blender --background --python table_code.py\n   ```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- Parametric control over object geometry\n- Easy experimentation with different dimensions\n- No need to re-scan or re-run inference\n\n---\n\n### 3. Batch Processing\n\n**Scenario:** You have 1000 point clouds and want to convert them all to code.\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1. **Create a text file** listing all NPZ file paths (`all_npz_files.txt`):\n   ```\n   /path/to/scan001.npz\n   /path/to/scan002.npz\n   /path/to/scan003.npz\n   ...\n   ```\n\n2. **Create a text file** for output paths (`all_output_codes.txt`):\n   ```\n   /output/code001.py\n   /output/code002.py\n   /output/code003.py\n   ...\n   ```\n\n3. **Run batch inference:**\n   ```bash\n   python inference_shape2code_point_input.py \\\n       --model_name MeshCoder/llama-3-models/Llama3.2-1B \\\n       --peft_model MeshCoder/llama-3-models/object_to_code_model \\\n       --npz_data_file 'all_npz_files.txt' \\\n       --code_save_file 'all_output_codes.txt' \\\n       --file_start_idx 0 \\\n       --file_end_idx 1000 \\\n       --batch_size 8 \\\n       --skip_existing_files True\n   ```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- Process large datasets efficiently\n- Resume interrupted processing with `skip_existing_files`\n- Control processing range with `file_start_idx` and `file_end_idx`\n\n---\n\n## Inference Guide\n\n### Data Preparation\n\nMeshCoder expects input data in **NPZ format** with the following structure:\n\n```python\n{\n    'points': np.array,   # Shape: (N, 3), normalized to [-1, 1]\n    'normals': np.array   # Shape: (N, 3), normalized\n}\n```\n\n**Sample datasets:**\n- **In-distribution (Infinigen)**: [sample.npz](https://huggingface.co/datasets/InternRobotics/MeshCoderDataset/blob/main/sample.npz)\n- **Out-of-distribution (TRELLIS)**: [ood_samples.npz](https://huggingface.co/datasets/InternRobotics/MeshCoderDataset/blob/main/ood_samples.npz)\n\nDownload and place in the `data/` folder.\n\n---\n\n### Single GPU Inference\n\nFor processing on a single GPU:\n\n```bash\ncd recipes/inference/local_inference/custom_inference\n\npython inference_shape2code_point_input.py \\\n    --model_name MeshCoder/llama-3-models/Llama3.2-1B \\\n    --peft_model MeshCoder/llama-3-models/object_to_code_model \\\n    --file_start_idx 0 \\\n    --file_end_idx 10000000 \\\n    --npz_data_file 'data/sample.npz' \\\n    --code_save_file 'res/code.py' \\\n    --skip_existing_files True \\\n    --batch_size 8 \\\n    --visualization \\\n    --do_sample False \\\n    --max_new_tokens 2048 \\\n    --zero_out_normals True\n```\n\n**Key Parameters:**\n- `--batch_size`: Number of samples processed simultaneously (adjust based on GPU memory)\n- `--visualization`: Enable to generate visual outputs\n- `--max_new_tokens`: Maximum length of generated code\n- `--zero_out_normals`: Set to True if normals are unreliable or missing\n\n---\n\n### Multi-GPU Inference\n\nFor faster processing across multiple GPUs/nodes:\n\n```bash\ncd recipes/inference/local_inference/custom_inference\n\n# Set environment variables (adjust for your cluster)\nexport NODE_RANK=0\nexport MASTER_ADDR=127.0.0.1\nexport MASTER_PORT=29500\n\n# Run with 4 nodes × 8 GPUs = 32 GPUs total\ntorchrun \\\n    --nnodes=4 \\\n    --nproc_per_node=8 \\\n    --node_rank=$NODE_RANK \\\n    --master_addr=$MASTER_ADDR \\\n    --master_port=$MASTER_PORT \\\n    inference_shape2code_point_input.py \\\n    --model_name MeshCoder/llama-3-models/Llama3.2-1B \\\n    --peft_model MeshCoder/llama-3-models/object_to_code_model \\\n    --file_start_idx 0 \\\n    --file_end_idx 10000000 \\\n    --npz_data_file 'data/sample.npz' \\\n    --code_save_file 'res/code.py' \\\n    --skip_existing_files True \\\n    --batch_size 8 \\\n    --visualization \\\n    --do_sample False \\\n    --max_new_tokens 2048 \\\n    --zero_out_normals True\n```\n\n**Cluster Configuration:**\n- Set `NODE_RANK` uniquely for each node (0, 1, 2, 3, ...)\n- Ensure `MASTER_ADDR` points to the main node's IP\n- Choose an available `MASTER_PORT`\n\n---\n\n## Training Guide\n\n### Dataset Preparation\n\n1. Download the [MeshCoder Dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/InternRobotics/MeshCoderDataset)\n\n2. Organize files as follows:\n   ```\n   MeshCoder/src/llama_recipes/datasets/shape2code_dataset/object2code/MeshCoderData/\n   ├── train.json\n   ├── val.json\n   ├── test.json\n   ├── dataset_train/  (unzipped point cloud files)\n   └── dataset_val/    (unzipped point cloud files)\n   ```\n\n3. Unzip all point cloud archives in `dataset_train/` and `dataset_val/`\n\n---\n\n### Single GPU Training\n\nTrain from scratch on a single GPU:\n\n```bash\npython -m llama_recipes.finetuning_shape2code \\\n    --model_name llama-3-models/Llama3.2-1B \\\n    --use_peft \\\n    --peft_method lora \\\n    --lora_config.r 16 \\\n    --shape_tokenizer_config llama-3-models/object_to_code_model/config.yaml \\\n    --output_dir llama-3-models/object_to_code_model_exp_1 \\\n    --dataset_version simplify_dataset \\\n    --max_sequence_length 5000 \\\n    --max_cd_loss 0.005 \\\n    --batching_strategy padding \\\n    --gradient_accumulation_steps 4 \\\n    --batch_size_training 2 \\\n    --num_workers_dataloader 8 \\\n    --val_batch_size 4 \\\n    --batch_sampler None \\\n    --num_epochs 10 \\\n    --shape_tokenizer_lr_scheduler_step_size 100 \\\n    --lr_scheduler_step_size 100 \\\n    --lr 1e-4 \\\n    --shape_tokenizer_lr 1e-4 \\\n    --dataset shape2code_dataset \\\n    --data_dir 'object2code/MeshCoderData' \\\n    --num_proc 32 \\\n    --num_samples 'train: -1; validation: -1; test: -1' \\\n    --dataset_labels 'infinigen' \\\n    --visualization True\n```\n\n**Fine-tuning from pretrained checkpoint:**\n\nAdd these flags to continue from the provided checkpoint:\n```bash\n--resume_training_dir llama-3-models/object_to_code_model \\\n--load_optimizer_state_dict\n```\n\n---\n\n### Multi-GPU Training\n\nTrain on multiple GPUs with FSDP (Fully Sharded Data Parallel):\n\n```bash\nexport NODE_RANK=0\nexport MASTER_ADDR=127.0.0.1\nexport MASTER_PORT=29500\n\ntorchrun \\\n    --nnodes=4 \\\n    --nproc_per_node=8 \\\n    --node_rank=$NODE_RANK \\\n    --master_addr=$MASTER_ADDR \\\n    --master_port=$MASTER_PORT \\\n    -m llama_recipes.finetuning_shape2code \\\n    --enable_fsdp \\\n    --model_name llama-3-models/Llama3.2-1B \\\n    --use_peft \\\n    --peft_method lora \\\n    --lora_config.r 16 \\\n    --shape_tokenizer_config llama-3-models/object_to_code_model/config.yaml \\\n    --output_dir llama-3-models/object_to_code_model_exp_1 \\\n    --dataset_version simplify_dataset \\\n    --max_sequence_length 5000 \\\n    --max_cd_loss 0.005 \\\n    --batching_strategy padding \\\n    --gradient_accumulation_steps 4 \\\n    --batch_size_training 2 \\\n    --num_workers_dataloader 8 \\\n    --val_batch_size 4 \\\n    --num_epochs 10 \\\n    --lr 1e-4 \\\n    --shape_tokenizer_lr 1e-4 \\\n    --dataset shape2code_dataset \\\n    --data_dir 'object2code/MeshCoderData' \\\n    --num_proc 32 \\\n    --num_samples 'train: -1; validation: -1; test: -1' \\\n    --dataset_labels 'infinigen' \\\n    --visualization True\n```\n\n---\n\n## Advanced Topics\n\n### Out-of-Distribution Generalization\n\nMeshCoder can reconstruct certain out-of-distribution (OOD) samples, demonstrating generalization capabilities. However, performance may degrade when point cloud distributions significantly differ from training data (Infinigen).\n\n**Testing OOD data:**\n```bash\npython inference_shape2code_point_input.py \\\n    --npz_data_file 'data/ood_samples.npz' \\\n    --code_save_file 'res/ood_code.py' \\\n    ...\n```\n\n**Future work** will focus on improving OOD generalization.\n\n---\n\n### Customizing Generated Code\n\nThe generated Blender scripts are human-readable and follow standard Python conventions. You can:\n\n- **Modify parameters**: Change dimensions, colors, materials\n- **Add features**: Insert additional Blender operations\n- **Combine objects**: Merge multiple generated scripts\n- **Export formats**: Add export commands for OBJ, STL, etc.\n\nExample modification:\n```python\n# Original generated code\nbpy.ops.mesh.primitive_cube_add(size=2, location=(0, 0, 0))\n\n# Modified code with material\nbpy.ops.mesh.primitive_cube_add(size=3, location=(1, 0, 0))\nmat = bpy.data.materials.new(name=\"CustomMaterial\")\nmat.diffuse_color = (1, 0, 0, 1)  # Red color\nbpy.context.object.data.materials.append(mat)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### Common Issues\n\n**1. `bpy` installation fails**\n```bash\n# Solution: Download wheel manually\nwget https://download.blender.org/pypi/bpy/bpy-4.0.0-cp310-cp310-manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl\npip install bpy-4.0.0-cp310-cp310-manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl\n```\n\n**2. CUDA out of memory**\n```bash\n# Solution: Reduce batch size\n--batch_size 4  # or lower\n```\n\n**3. Model files not found**\n```bash\n# Solution: Verify directory structure\nMeshCoder/llama-3-models/\n├── Llama3.2-1B/          # Base model\n└── object_to_code_model/  # LoRA weights + tokenizer\n    ├── config.yaml\n    ├── shape_tokenizer.pt\n    ├── adapter_config.json\n    └── adapter_model.safetensors\n```\n\n**4. Point cloud normalization issues**\n\nEnsure your point clouds are normalized to `[-1, 1]`:\n```python\npoints = (points - points.min(axis=0)) / (points.max(axis=0) - points.min(axis=0))  # [0, 1]\npoints = points * 2 - 1  # [-1, 1]\n```\n\n**5. Poor reconstruction quality**\n\n- Ensure sufficient point density (recommended: 2048+ points)\n- Check if normals are accurate (use `--zero_out_normals True` if uncertain)\n- Verify point cloud is centered and normalized\n\n---\n\n## Join Us\n\nWe are seeking engineers, interns, researchers, and PhD candidates interested in 3D content generation. If interested, please send your resume to **lvzhaoyang@pjlab.org.cn**.\n\n---\n\n## Citation\n\nIf you find our work helpful, please consider citing:\n\n```bibtex\n@article{dai2025meshcoder,\n  title={MeshCoder: LLM-Powered Structured Mesh Code Generation from Point Clouds},\n  author={Dai, Bingquan and Luo, Li Ray and Tang, Qihong and Wang, Jie and Lian, Xinyu and Xu, Hao and Qin, Minghan and Xu, Xudong and Dai, Bo and Wang, Haoqian and others},\n  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2508.14879},\n  year={2025}\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Acknowledgement\n\nWe thank all authors behind these repositories for their excellent work:\n- [Llama](https://github.com/meta-llama/llama-cookbook)\n- [TripoSR](https://github.com/VAST-AI-Research/TripoSR)\n\n---\n\n## License\n\nThis project uses the Llama 2 Community License Agreement. See the original repository for details.\n\n---\n\n## Contact\n\nFor questions and feedback:\n- Open an issue on [GitHub](https://github.com/InternRobotics/MeshCoder/issues)\n- Visit our [project page](https://daibingquan.github.io/MeshCoder)\n- Email: lvzhaoyang@pjlab.org.cn\n"
